{
  "version": 2,
  "_doc": "Mutually exclusive variant groups (axes). Each value lists English and Arabic phrases; phrases are matched on whole tokens after catalog_match.text_norm normalisation, longest phrase first. 'unmarked' lists the values that do NOT make a candidate suspicious when the target SKU does not state that axis (e.g. a retailer title adding 'Full Fat' to 'Almarai Fresh Milk'). 'soft_groups' lists sets of values of one axis that describe closely related but distinct lines (Diet vs Zero Sugar vs Sugar Free): a difference inside one set is a soft conflict (never tier 1, never auto-publish) instead of a hard reject, because retailers word the same line differently. The form axis has no unmarked values: fresh, long-life, frozen and powder are different SKUs, so a candidate stating a form the SKU does not state is capped below tier 1. The medium axis is the oil or liquid a product is packed in or made of (tuna in sunflower oil vs olive oil vs water; sunflower vs corn oil): different SKUs, so it has no unmarked value. 'contexts' names token sets; an axis's 'context_values' ({context: {value: [phrases]}}) are matched only when the text, or the SKU the text is compared with, contains a token of that context. 'White' is a meat grade on a tuna can and a colour anywhere else; 'water' is the packing medium of tuna and the product itself for a bottle. At equal phrase length a context-bound phrase wins ('light' on a tuna can is the meat grade, not low fat). 'fries_cut' (thin, crinkle, steak, ...) and 'cheese_form' (shredded, sliced, ...) are context-bound the same way: 'thin' is a cut only next to fries or potato, 'block' a form only next to cheese. Their default forms (straight-cut fries, a cheese block) are unmarked. An axis's 'context_unmarked' ({context: [values]}) lists values that are the normal state of products in that context, so stating them is not suspicious: fries, paratha, nuggets and shawarma are sold frozen, and 'Frozen French Fries' neither caps tier 1 nor warns the reviewer for 'AIDA FRENCH FRIES'. The protein axis (beef, chicken, mutton, lamb, fish) is read only next to a product made of one of them ('meat_products': luncheon meat, masala, burgers, sausages; live run 2026-10-03: 'ZWAN BEEF LUNCHEON MEAT' scored the Zwan Chicken listing tier 1, 'ALLDE MEAT MASALA' the Allde Chicken Masala one). An axis's 'bound_values' ({context: {value: [phrases]}}) count only right next to a token of that context: the next word (across 'and' and the axis's other values: 'Meat & Chicken Masala') or the word before ('بهارات لحم'), never after a 'bound_not_after' word. So 'meat' is a protein only in 'meat masala', 'meat spice', 'meat burger', never in 'luncheon meat' or 'luncheon meat with spices'. An axis's 'generic' values ({generic: [the specific values it covers]}) are a family: 'meat' covers beef, mutton and lamb. A generic value against values it covers is a soft conflict, never a hard one; on the SKU side a covered value stated next to it replaces it ('Mutton Meat Masala' is mutton); a listing keeps it ('Meat Masala for Mutton & Lamb' is that SKU's 'meat masala'). An axis with 'never_in_brand' reads no value inside a brand name ('Lamb Weston' fries are not lamb).",
